      },
      "worked": "The circle has radius $2$. At $x=1$, its intersections are $(1,\\sqrt{3})$ and $(1,-\\sqrt{3})$. Each outer segment subtends an angle $\\theta$ at the centre.\n$$\\begin{align*}r&=2\\\\y&=\\pm\\sqrt{4-1}=\\pm\\sqrt{3}\\\\\\theta&=2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\end{align*}$$\nUsing the segment-area formula,\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{segment}}&=\\frac12(2)^2\\left(\\frac{2\\pi}{3}-\\sin\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\A_{\\text{segment}}&=\\frac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\end{align*}$$\nThe lines $x=1$ and $x=-1$ cut off equal segments. Subtract both from the circle:\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=4\\pi-2\\left(\\frac{4\\pi}{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\right)\\\\A&=2\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{4\\pi}{3}\\text{ square units}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
